<!-- What Campaigns Include Section -->
<section class="content-section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="content-block">
            <h2>What Organic Campaigns Include</h2>
            <p>Organic visibility work covers everything that affects whether your site appears in Google results. I work across three main areas:</p>
            
            <h3>Technical Foundations</h3>
            <p>The technical layer is the foundation. If Google can't crawl your site properly, content and links won't matter. Technical work includes site speed improvements, mobile usability, crawl budget management, schema markup, XML sitemaps, and fixing errors that prevent indexing. Most business sites I encounter have at least a few technical issues holding them back.</p>
            
            <h3>Content and Targeting</h3>
            <p>Content is what ranks. I figure out what your potential customers are actually searching for, then create or improve pages to match those queries. This isn't about stuffing phrases into paragraphs. It's about having pages that answer the questions people ask Google. A pipefitter in Parramatta needs different content than a lawyer in the CBD, even though both want more traffic from search.</p>
            
            <h3>Links and Authority</h3>
            <p>Links from other sites tell Google your site is worth ranking. I build links through content outreach, local business connections, and industry relationships. Not directories, not spammy guest posts, not PBNs. Actual links from real sites that send traffic as well as pass authority.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- How Campaigns Work Section -->
<section class="content-section alt-bg">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="content-block">
            <h2>How Campaigns Work</h2>
            <p>Every campaign starts with an audit. I look at your current rankings, technical setup, content, and backlink profile. Then I work out what's going to move the needle fastest for your specific situation.</p>
            
            <p>Some sites need technical fixes first. A site that takes six seconds to load or has broken pages isn't going to rank well regardless of content quality. Other sites are technically fine but have thin content or weak link profiles. The audit tells me where to focus.</p>
            
            <p>From there it's monthly work. I pick the highest-impact tasks each month, do them, track results, and adjust. You get a report at the end of each month showing what was done and what moved. If something isn't working I'll say so and we'll try something else.</p>
            
            <p>Campaigns are month-to-month. No lock-in contracts. If you want to stop you give 30 days notice. Most clients stay because they're seeing results, not because they're locked in.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- For Local Businesses Section -->
<section class="content-section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="content-block">
            <h2>Campaigns for Businesses Across the Metro Area</h2>
            <p>The metro area has its own search dynamics. Competition is high in most industries. The CBD, North Shore, Parramatta and other commercial centres each have different patterns and competitor landscapes.</p>
            
            <p>Geographic targeting matters for any business serving customers in specific areas. A dentist in Chatswood doesn't need to rank across the entire region, they need to rank for people searching nearby. A commercial lawyer in the CBD has different requirements again.</p>
            
            <p>I've worked with businesses across the CBD, inner west, eastern suburbs, north shore, northern beaches, Parramatta, Hills District and western suburbs. Understanding how people search by suburb, region and "near me" terms in each area informs the approach I take.</p>
            
            <p>For businesses with physical locations, Google Business Profile work is part of the mix. For service-area businesses like trades, I focus on service pages and content that targets the areas you actually work in.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

